                s("Key PMBOK 8 Terminology — Know These Definitions", tableHeaders = listOf("Term", "PMBOK 8 Definition"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Artifact", "A template, document, output, or project deliverable. A product is an artifact that is produced, quantifiable, and can be an end item or component."), listOf("Deliverable", "Any unique and verifiable product, result, or capability to perform a service that is required to be produced to complete a process, phase, or project."), listOf("Outcome", "An end result or consequence of a process or project. Outcomes can include outputs and artifacts but have broader intent — focused on benefits and value."), listOf("Value", "The worth, importance, or usefulness of something. Different stakeholders perceive value differently — financial, social, environmental, reputational."), listOf("Benefit", "A gain realised by the organisation and beneficiaries through project outputs and resulting outcomes."), listOf("Disbenefit", "A negative consequence of project outputs — must be identified and managed alongside benefits (new in PMBOK 8 vocabulary)."), listOf("Project Management Team", "Members of the project team directly involved in project management activities (broader than just the PM)."), listOf("Development Approach", "The method used to create and evolve the product, service, or result — predictive, adaptive, or hybrid."), listOf("Life Cycle", "The series of phases a project passes through from start to completion. Different from the development approach."), listOf("Tailoring", "The deliberate adaptation of the PM approach, governance, and processes to make them more suitable for the given environment."))),
                s("Project Success — PMBOK 8 Criteria", "PMBOK 8 defines project success beyond the traditional iron triangle:"),
                s("Project Success — PMBOK 8 Criteria", tableHeaders = listOf("Traditional (PMBOK 6)", "Modern (PMBOK 8)"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("On time", "Delivered when needed — cadence matters more than a single date"), listOf("On budget", "Investment justified by value delivered — not just spend control"), listOf("Within scope", "Delivered the RIGHT scope — outcomes achieved, not just outputs"), listOf("—", "Benefits realised (during and after project)"), listOf("—", "Stakeholder satisfaction (including end users)"), listOf("—", "Sustainability impact (people, profit, planet)"), listOf("—", "Organisational learning captured"))),
                s("Worked example — The 6 Principles — Applied to a Real Project Situation", "Project: Implementing AI-powered credit scoring for a microfinance institution serving rural borrowers\n\nPrinciple 1 — Adopt a Holistic View:\n\nThe team notices the AI model is trained on urban credit bureau data. Rural borrowers have different financial patterns (agricultural income cycles, informal credit). A holistic view reveals the model may systematically deny credit to exactly the customers the institution wants to serve.\n\n→ Expand the model training to include rural financial behaviour data before launch.\n\nPrinciple 2 — Focus on Value:\n\nThe project's KPI is \"number of loan applications processed per day.\" The PM flags that processing 500 applications efficiently but approving 400 incorrect ones destroys value. Reframe the KPI to \"default rate\" and \"customer satisfaction\" — the actual value drivers.\n\nPrinciple 3 — Embed Quality:\n\nRather than testing the AI model only at the end, add bias-testing checkpoints after every training iteration. Make \"fairness metrics across demographic groups\" part of the Definition of Done for each model version.\n\nPrinciple 4 — Be an Accountable Leader:\n\nWhen a team member discovers the model makes errors on agricultural borrowers, the PM does not blame the data scientist. She takes ownership: \"We did not include this in our test cases. I own that. Here is how we fix it.\" This creates safety to surface problems early.\n\nPrinciple 5 — Integrate Sustainability:\n\nThe institution serves 50,000 rural borrowers. A biased model that denies credit to creditworthy rural women would have profound social impact (disbenefits). The PM documents both the financial benefit AND the social risk in the Benefits Management Plan.\n\nPrinciple 6 — Build an Empowered Culture:\n\nThe junior data scientist noticed the bias issue but was afraid to raise it. After the PM's Principle 4 response, he feels safe to raise two more concerns in the next sprint. The team's best ideas are now accessible.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Focus Areas (Process Groups) — What They Look Like in Practice", "Project: Building a mobile-first insurance claims app (hybrid approach)\n\nINITIATING:\n\n• Sponsor signs Project Charter authorising ₹2.5 crore budget\n\n• PM and sponsor agree on success criteria: \"70% of claims submitted digitally within 6 months of launch\"\n\n• Initial stakeholder identification: policyholders, claim adjusters, IT team, legal/compliance, call centre\n\n• Assumption Log created: \"Average claim takes 7 days to process today; target is 3 days\"\n\nPLANNING:\n\n• PM Plan integrated: scope, schedule, cost, quality, risk, resource, communications, stakeholder, procurement plans\n\n• Baselines: WBS approved (22 epics → 180 user stories); Schedule baseline: 18 months; Cost baseline: ₹2.2 crore (₹30L contingency above)\n\n• Risk Register: 18 risks identified; 4 high-priority; contingency plans for top 3\n\nEXECUTING:\n\n• Sprint 1–3: Claims submission flow (predictive infrastructure running in parallel)\n\n• Manage Quality: two UX usability tests with actual policyholders; team adjusts form flow after feedback\n\n• Vendor (OCR engine for document scanning) contracted using CPIF to incentivise accuracy\n\nMONITORING & CONTROLLING (runs throughout):\n\n• Sprint 6: SPI = 0.91 (slightly behind). PM analyzes: one backend developer out sick for 2 weeks. Corrective action: redistribute tasks; no change request needed as contingency buffer absorbs.\n\n• Sprint 11: Compliance team raises new RBI data localisation requirement. Change request raised; +₹18L, +3 weeks approved by CCB.\n\nCLOSING:\n\n• UAT completed; 94% of test cases passed (above 90% threshold)\n\n• Formal acceptance signed by Head of Claims\n\n• Lessons learned workshop: 3 retrospective improvement ideas adopted into the PMO's standard playbook\n\n• Team disbanded; transition document handed to operations\n\n• Benefits tracking begun: adoption rate 34% in Month 1 (target 70% by Month 6)", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("PMBOK 8 vs PMBOK 7 vs PMBOK 6 — Complete Comparison Table", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "PMBOK 6 (2017)", "PMBOK 7 (2021)", "PMBOK 8 (2025)"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Core Structure", "49 Processes + 10 Knowledge Areas", "12 Principles + 8 Performance Domains", "6 Principles + 7 Domains + 40 Processes"), listOf("Process Groups", "5 explicit (Initiating through Closing)", "Removed — described as a separate guide", "Reintroduced as 5 Focus Areas within guide"), listOf("ITTOs", "Central — detailed inputs, tools, outputs for all 49 processes", "Removed from main guide", "Reintegrated into domain processes"), listOf("Quality", "Separate Knowledge Area (Ch.8)", "Within Delivery domain", "Within Governance domain"), listOf("Communications", "Separate KA (Ch.10)", "Within Stakeholders domain", "Merged into Stakeholders domain"), listOf("Procurement", "Separate KA (Ch.12)", "Distributed across domains", "Across Resources + Governance"), listOf("AI Guidance", "Not mentioned", "Brief mention", "Dedicated task (Business Environment B7)"), listOf("Sustainability", "Not explicit", "Brief mention", "Dedicated principle (Principle 5) + dedicated task (B8)"), listOf("Lifecycle Bias", "Predictive-heavy", "Agnostic", "Fully agnostic, agile-forward"), listOf("Agile Coverage", "Brief appendix", "Central", "Integrated throughout all domains"), listOf("Process Count", "49", "~14 practices", "40 non-prescriptive processes"), listOf("Edition", "6th (2017)", "7th (2021)", "8th (2025)"))),
                s("All 40 PMBOK 8 Processes — Full Reference", tableHeaders = listOf("#", "Process Name", "Domain", "Focus Area", "Primary Output"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("1", "Initiate Project or Phase", "Governance", "Initiating", "Project Charter, Initial Assumption Log"), listOf("2", "Integrate and Align Project Plans", "Governance", "Planning", "Project Management Plan (integrated)"), listOf("3", "Plan Sourcing Strategy", "Governance", "Planning", "Procurement Strategy, Make-or-Buy decisions"), listOf("4", "Manage Project Execution", "Governance", "Executing", "Deliverables, Work Performance Data"), listOf("5", "Manage Quality Assurance", "Governance", "Executing", "Quality Reports, Process Improvements"), listOf("6", "Manage Project Knowledge", "Governance", "Executing", "Lessons Learned Register updates, OPA updates"), listOf("7", "Monitor and Control Project Performance", "Governance", "M&C", "Work Performance Reports, Change Requests"), listOf("8", "Assess and Implement Changes", "Governance", "M&C", "Approved Changes, Change Log, Plan Updates"), listOf("9", "Close Project or Phase", "Governance", "Closing", "Final Report, Closed Contracts, OPA updates"), listOf("10", "Plan Scope Management", "Scope", "Planning", "Scope Management Plan"), listOf("11", "Elicit and Analyse Requirements", "Scope", "Planning", "Requirements Documentation, RTM"), listOf("12", "Define Scope", "Scope", "Planning", "Project Scope Statement"), listOf("13", "Develop Scope Structure", "Scope", "Planning", "WBS, WBS Dictionary (Scope Baseline)"), listOf("14", "Validate Scope", "Scope", "M&C", "Accepted Deliverables"), listOf("15", "Monitor and Control Scope", "Scope", "M&C", "Work Performance Information, Change Requests"), listOf("16", "Plan Schedule Management", "Schedule", "Planning", "Schedule Management Plan"), listOf("17", "Develop Schedule", "Schedule", "Planning", "Project Schedule, Schedule Baseline"), listOf("18", "Monitor and Control Schedule", "Schedule", "M&C", "Work Performance Information, Change Requests"), listOf("19", "Plan Financial Management", "Finance", "Planning", "Financial Management Plan"), listOf("20", "Estimate Costs", "Finance", "Planning", "Cost Estimates, Basis of Estimates"), listOf("21", "Develop Budget", "Finance", "Planning", "Cost Baseline, Project Funding Requirements"), listOf("22", "Monitor and Control Finances", "Finance", "M&C", "Work Performance Information, Change Requests"), listOf("23", "Identify Stakeholders", "Stakeholders", "Initiating", "Stakeholder Register"), listOf("24", "Plan Stakeholder Engagement", "Stakeholders", "Planning", "Stakeholder Engagement Plan"), listOf("25", "Plan Communications Management", "Stakeholders", "Planning", "Communications Management Plan"), listOf("26", "Manage Stakeholder Engagement", "Stakeholders", "Executing", "Change Requests, OPA updates"), listOf("27", "Manage Communications", "Stakeholders", "Executing", "Project Communications, OPA updates"), listOf("28", "Monitor Stakeholder Engagement", "Stakeholders", "M&C", "Work Performance Information, Change Requests"), listOf("29", "Monitor Communications", "Stakeholders", "M&C", "Work Performance Information"), listOf("30", "Plan Resource Management", "Resources", "Planning", "Resource Management Plan"), listOf("31", "Estimate Resources", "Resources", "Planning", "Resource Requirements, Resource Breakdown Structure"), listOf("32", "Acquire Resources", "Resources", "Executing", "Physical Resource Assignments, Project Team Assignments"), listOf("33", "Lead the Team", "Resources", "Executing", "Team Performance Assessments, Change Requests"), listOf("34", "Monitor and Control Resourcing", "Resources", "M&C", "Work Performance Information, Change Requests"), listOf("35", "Plan Risk Management", "Risk", "Planning", "Risk Management Plan"), listOf("36", "Identify Risks", "Risk", "Planning", "Risk Register"), listOf("37", "Perform Risk Analysis", "Risk", "Planning", "Risk Report, Priority Risk List"), listOf("38", "Plan Risk Responses", "Risk", "Planning", "Risk Register updates, Risk Report updates"), listOf("39", "Implement Risk Responses", "Risk", "Executing", "Change Requests, Risk Register updates"), listOf("40", "Monitor Risks", "Risk", "M&C", "Work Performance Information, Change Requests"))),
                s("All 40 PMBOK 8 Processes — Full Reference", "Only the GOVERNANCE domain has processes in ALL five Focus Areas. All other domains skip Initiating (except Stakeholders) and Closing. This is frequently tested.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("PMBOK 8 Principles — Deep Dive with Exam Application", tableHeaders = listOf("Principle", "Core Concept", "Exam Application", "Anti-Pattern (wrong answer)"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("1. Adopt a Holistic View", "Think systemically — consider all stakeholders, environment, interrelationships, and second-order effects", "When a question shows a decision that optimises one metric but harms others, the holistic view would catch this", "Focusing only on cost/schedule without considering quality, stakeholder impact, or sustainability"), listOf("2. Focus on Value", "Every decision, activity, and deliverable must be justified by the value it creates — for stakeholders and the organisation", "When asked \"should we continue this project?\" — evaluate value, not sunk costs", "Measuring success by triple constraint alone — on time, on budget, but zero adoption = failure"), listOf("3. Embed Quality", "Quality is planned and built in — not inspected in at the end. Continuous improvement.", "Prevention cost questions — always prefer preventing over fixing. DoD in agile = quality built in", "Allowing a deliverable to ship that \"looks fine\" without verification against specifications"), listOf("4. Be an Accountable Leader", "Lead with integrity. Share leadership — any team member can lead. Foster psychological safety.", "When asked about leadership style: servant leadership for empowered teams. Own your mistakes.", "Autocratic decision-making as default. Blame culture. Hiding mistakes."), listOf("5. Integrate Sustainability", "People, profit, planet. Short-term and long-term. Consider community, society, future generations.", "Any question about environmental impact, social impact, ESG, or disbenefits connects here", "Ignoring negative social/environmental consequences because they are \"outside project scope\""), listOf("6. Build an Empowered Culture", "Mutual trust, clear roles, collaborative objectives. Empowered teams maximise value.", "Team charter questions. Autonomy vs control. Empowerment vs micromanagement.", "Centralising all decisions with the PM. Not involving team in planning or estimation."))),
                s("Delivery Cadence — Choosing the Right Approach", tableHeaders = listOf("Factor", "Predictive", "Iterative", "Incremental", "Agile", "Hybrid"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Requirements clarity", "Very clear and stable", "Partially clear; may evolve", "Clear about WHAT; HOW is refined", "Unclear; emerges over time", "Mixed — some clear, some unclear"), listOf("Change rate", "Low — changes are expensive", "Moderate — planned iterations", "Low per increment", "High — change is expected and welcome", "Varies by component"), listOf("Delivery timing", "Single delivery at end", "Multiple versions of same deliverable", "Partial but usable deliverables over time", "Frequent small deliveries, always usable", "Different cadences per component"), listOf("Stakeholder involvement", "At milestones/gates", "At end of each iteration", "At each increment delivery", "Continuous collaboration", "Varies by component"), listOf("Risk approach", "Identify and plan upfront", "Revisit each iteration", "Reduce risk incrementally", "Risk managed per sprint", "Both formal and iterative"), listOf("Best for", "Construction, manufacturing, regulated", "R&D, software prototyping", "Large software releases, hardware", "Software, services, innovation", "Complex projects with multiple workstreams"), listOf("PMBOK 8 example", "Building a bridge", "Drug development phases", "ERP module releases", "Mobile app development", "Bank system with hardware + software"))),
                s("Organisational Governance Structures and Their Impact", tableHeaders = listOf("Structure", "Definition", "PM Authority", "Resource Control", "Typical Industries"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Organic/Simple", "No formal structure; owner manages everything", "None — owner makes all decisions", "Owner controls all", "Micro-businesses, family businesses"), listOf("Functional", "Organised by function (IT, Finance, HR); functional managers rule", "Little to none; PM is coordinator", "Functional Manager controls", "Manufacturing, government, military"), listOf("Weak Matrix", "PM role exists but FM is dominant", "Low — PM is coordinator or facilitator", "Functional Manager dominant", "Large corporations in transition"), listOf("Balanced Matrix", "Power shared between PM and FM", "Moderate — shared authority", "Negotiated between PM and FM", "Consulting firms, professional services"), listOf("Strong Matrix", "PM has significant authority; PM is full-time", "Moderate to High", "PM dominant", "IT companies, engineering firms"), listOf("Project-Oriented", "Organisation structured around projects; PMs have full authority", "High to Almost Total", "PM controls", "Construction, aerospace, defence"), listOf("PMO Structure", "PMO manages the portfolio; PM staff from PMO", "High to Almost Total", "PMO controls allocation", "Large enterprise, programme management"), listOf("Virtual", "Geographically dispersed; technology-enabled", "Varies", "Technology-dependent", "Global IT, outsourcing, modern enterprises"))),
            )
        ),
        Chapter(
            "mindset",
            "🧠 PM Mindset — The Most Important Section",
            "PM Mindset · A.R.T. Framework · Conflict Resolution · Ethics · Cultural Intelligence",
            "People domain",
            listOf(
                s("Danger", "The mindset section is where most PMP candidates lose marks. These questions test HOW you think as a PM, not what you know. Study this section deeply.", SectionKind.DANGER),
                s("PM Characteristics — Core Behaviours", tableHeaders = listOf("Always Do", "Never Do"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Be proactive — anticipate problems before they occur", "Take immediate drastic action without analyzing first"), listOf("Embody servant leadership — serve the team, remove impediments", "Fire, terminate, or remove team members as a first response"), listOf("Communicate proactively and transparently with all stakeholders", "Bypass steps, processes, or documentation for any reason"), listOf("Involve stakeholders in decision-making actively", "Pass problems to stakeholders — bring solutions, not problems"), listOf("Act after collecting complete and accurate data", "Delegate PM responsibilities to team members or others"), listOf("Resolve conflicts at the lowest possible level first", "Ignore or avoid stakeholder feedback or concerns"), listOf("Follow the process — even under pressure from authority", "Implement changes without CCB approval — even \"small\" ones"), listOf("Stop all work when a safety issue is identified", "Let scope creep go undocumented or uncontrolled"), listOf("Escalate only when issue is beyond PM authority", "Escalate before attempting resolution at the team level"), listOf("Keep lessons learned continuously — not just at project end", "Document lessons learned only at project closure"))),
                s("A.R.T. Framework — Answer ANY Situational Question", "When a question describes a problem and asks what the PM should do, follow this mental sequence before selecting your answer:\n\nAAssess / AnalyzeCollect data. Identify root cause. Evaluate situation with team. Review what is happening before any action.\n →\n RReviewReview the Project Management Plan, scope, schedule, budget. Are documents aligned? What do they prescribe?\n →\n TTake ActionMeet stakeholders, execute risk response, raise change requests. Practical, achievable steps aligned to the plan."),
                s("A.R.T. Framework — Answer ANY Situational Question", "If an answer option says \"immediately implement X\" or \"tell the sponsor right away\" — it is almost certainly wrong. ALWAYS assess and review FIRST, then act.", SectionKind.WARN),
                s("A.R.T. Applied — Examples", tableHeaders = listOf("Situation", "Wrong Answer (Too Fast)", "Right Answer (A.R.T.)"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("A team member reports the project is behind schedule", "Immediately crash the schedule (add resources)", "Analyze the variance (EVM) → Review the schedule baseline → Determine corrective action options → Communicate to stakeholders"), listOf("A stakeholder raises an unexpected new requirement", "Add it to scope right away since they are an important stakeholder", "Assess impact on scope/schedule/cost → Review change control process → Raise formal change request → CCB decides"), listOf("A key team member resigns mid-project", "Immediately hire a replacement consultant", "Assess impact on schedule and knowledge → Review resource management plan → Evaluate internal options → Then consider external if needed"), listOf("A vendor says they will miss a delivery milestone", "Terminate the contract immediately", "Assess impact on project → Review procurement contract and vendor plan → Meet with vendor face-to-face → Explore alternatives → Escalate if contractual breach"))),
                s("Risk vs. Issue — Critical Distinction", tableHeaders = listOf("Risk", "Issue"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Definition", "Uncertain event that MIGHT happen in the future", "Problem that HAS ALREADY occurred or is occurring now"), listOf("Keywords in question", "May, might, could potentially, if, possibility of", "Will, should, has happened, is happening, occurred"), listOf("First Action", "Add to Risk Register → Analyze → Plan Response", "Update Issue Log immediately"), listOf("Where documented", "Risk Register (NOT risk management plan)", "Issue Log"), listOf("After resolution", "N/A — ongoing monitoring", "Update Lessons Learned + OPA"))),
                s("Risk vs. Issue — Critical Distinction", "Remember: Risks are POTENTIAL — they go in the Risk Register. Issues are REAL and NOW — they go in the Issue Log. Do NOT confuse these two documents.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("Conflict Resolution — PMI Priority Order", tableHeaders = listOf("Rank", "Technique", "Description", "PMI View"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("1 ✅", "Collaborate / Problem Solve", "All parties work toward a win-win solution; addresses root cause", "ALWAYS the preferred answer"), listOf("2", "Compromise / Reconcile", "Each party gives something; partially satisfies both", "Good when full collaboration is not possible"), listOf("3", "Smooth / Accommodate", "Downplay differences; emphasise areas of agreement", "Temporary — does not fix root cause"), listOf("4", "Force / Direct", "One party's position wins; win-lose outcome", "Only in emergencies or time-critical situations"), listOf("5 ❌", "Withdraw / Avoid", "Ignore or postpone the conflict entirely", "Almost always wrong — never a long-term solution"))),
                s("Conflict Resolution — PMI Priority Order", "For conflict between two team members: meet with each party INDIVIDUALLY first, then bring them together. Face-to-face communication is always the preferred method.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Keyword-to-Action Map", tableHeaders = listOf("Keyword in Question", "PM Action"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Do / Do First / Do Next", "Assess impact → evaluate scope/time/cost/quality → involve stakeholders → take measured action"), listOf("Have Done / Should Have Done", "Proactive measures: detailed planning, risk process, regular stakeholder communication"), listOf("Not Do / Not Have Done", "Avoid: ignoring feedback, disregarding risks, scope creep, bypassing change control"), listOf("Never Do", "Never: fire/hire mid-project, terminate contracts without analysis, implement changes without CCB, ignore safety or ethics"), listOf("Least Likely / Most Likely", "Order from Most → Least important. Most Likely = highest priority action in the scenario"), listOf("Safety issue arises", "STOP all work immediately. Address safety first, then follow change control processes"), listOf("New law / regulation", "Update Risk Register → adjust project plans → ensure compliance is achieved"), listOf("Project closure", "Formal acceptance → Lessons Learned → Transition to Operations → Disband team"), listOf("Estimation needed", "The TEAM estimates — they have subject matter expertise (SME). PM does not estimate for them."), listOf("When to escalate?", "Only when issue is beyond PM authority AND internal resolution has been genuinely attempted"))),
                s("Traditional PM vs. Agile Scrum Master Mindset", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "Traditional PM", "Agile Scrum Master"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Team Leadership", "Directive; PM assigns tasks and controls work", "Servant leader; team self-organises and self-assigns"), listOf("Planning Approach", "Comprehensive upfront documentation required", "Adaptive; just enough; rolling wave; sprint-by-sprint"), listOf("Change Management", "Formal change requests through CCB; baselines protected", "PO reprioritises backlog; lightweight process; embraces change"), listOf("Stakeholder Engagement", "Identify and analyze at start; manage throughout", "Continuous collaboration; PO as primary stakeholder interface"), listOf("Handling Skill Gaps", "PM arranges training or resource replacement", "SM provides coaching and training resources; team coaches each other"), listOf("Performance Measurement", "On time, on budget, as specified (EVM)", "Value delivered; customer satisfaction; working product"), listOf("Project Closure", "Formal closure even for early termination", "Continuous delivery; retrospective reviews; no single \"closure\""))),
                s("The \"Most Likely Wrong Answer\" Patterns", "PMI exam writers use consistent patterns for distractor (wrong) answers. Learn to spot them:"),
                s("The \"Most Likely Wrong Answer\" Patterns", tableHeaders = listOf("Pattern", "Why It Is Wrong", "Example"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Immediate drastic action", "Skips assessment; violates A.R.T.", "\"Immediately terminate the vendor contract\""), listOf("Escalate first", "PM abdicates responsibility", "\"Report the issue to the sponsor right away\""), listOf("Ignore / do nothing", "Never proactive", "\"Continue as planned and monitor\""), listOf("Bypass process", "Violates governance", "\"Make the change without formal approval since it is small\""), listOf("Blame or remove people", "Violates servant leadership", "\"Replace the underperforming team member\""), listOf("Absolute language", "PM is contextual, not absolute", "\"Always require formal documentation for all decisions\""), listOf("PM does the work", "PM manages; team executes", "\"The PM should personally fix the defect\""), listOf("Reactive over proactive", "PMI wants anticipation", "\"Wait for the issue to become critical before acting\""), listOf("Decide alone", "PMI wants collaboration", "\"The PM should decide the technical approach\""))),
                s("Ethics — PMI Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct", "Ethics questions appear in all three domains. The PMI Code has four values. Always choose the option that upholds ALL four:"),
                s("Ethics — PMI Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct", tableHeaders = listOf("Value", "Definition", "Exam Application"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Responsibility", "Take ownership of decisions and consequences; report unethical conduct", "If you discover a problem, you own reporting it — even if inconvenient"), listOf("Respect", "Treat all people with dignity; listen to others; negotiate in good faith", "No discrimination; no bullying; respect cultural differences"), listOf("Fairness", "Make decisions impartially; disclose conflicts of interest; no favouritism", "Declare conflicts of interest; treat vendors equally in procurement"), listOf("Honesty", "Be truthful in communications and conduct; do not deceive", "Never misreport project status; never hide bad news from stakeholders"))),
                s("Ethics — PMI Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct", "Exam rule: When an ethics question offers a \"convenient\" option and an \"honest but difficult\" option — always choose the honest one. PMI never rewards convenience over integrity.", SectionKind.DANGER),
                s("Common Ethics Scenarios", tableHeaders = listOf("Scenario", "Correct Response"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("You discover a colleague is falsifying test results", "Report it through appropriate channels — do not confront alone or ignore"), listOf("A vendor offers you a gift during procurement", "Decline; report per organisational policy; ensure no conflict of interest"), listOf("Sponsor asks you to report project status as \"green\" when it is actually \"red\"", "Refuse; report accurately; explain consequences of misreporting"), listOf("You are asked to work in a country with different ethics norms", "Follow PMI Code of Ethics regardless of local practices; escalate if conflict"), listOf("You have a personal relationship with a bidding vendor", "Disclose the conflict of interest immediately; recuse from selection decision"), listOf("A team member from a different culture is being excluded", "Address the exclusion; foster inclusion; apply cultural intelligence"))),
                s("Emotional Intelligence (EI) — Tested in People Domain", tableHeaders = listOf("EI Component", "Definition", "PM Application"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Self-Awareness", "Understand your own emotions, strengths, weaknesses, and their impact", "Recognise when stress affects your decision-making"), listOf("Self-Regulation", "Control impulses and moods; think before acting", "Do not react angrily in a heated meeting; pause and assess"), listOf("Motivation", "Drive to achieve beyond money or status; commitment and optimism", "Maintain team morale during setbacks"), listOf("Empathy", "Understand others' emotions; treat people according to their needs", "Recognise when a team member is struggling personally"), listOf("Social Skills", "Manage relationships; build networks; find common ground", "Resolve conflicts; influence without authority"))),
                s("Cultural Intelligence — Virtual and Global Teams", "• Recognise differing communication styles (direct vs indirect, hierarchical vs flat)\n• Adapt communication frequency and format to cultural preferences\n• Avoid scheduling important meetings during religious or national holidays of team members\n• Use visual communication (diagrams, dashboards) to overcome language barriers\n• Never assume one culture's norms are \"correct\" — this is bias\n• Build a team charter that explicitly documents agreed norms respecting all cultures"),
                s("Worked example — A.R.T. Framework — 5 Scenario Walk-throughs", "Scenario 1: \"The project is 3 weeks behind schedule.\"\n\nAssess: Which activities are delayed? Is it the critical path? What caused the delay? (Resource shortage? Underestimation? Dependency?)\n\nReview: What does the Schedule Management Plan say about recovery? Is there contingency? What does the Risk Register say (was this risk identified)?\n\nTake Action: Determine options (fast-track, crash, re-scope). Present to sponsor with impact. Raise change request if baseline needs to change.\n\n→ WRONG first action: \"Immediately crash the critical path.\" (Skips assessment of WHY and what options exist)\n\nScenario 2: \"A team member finds a serious defect in a module that was already accepted by the customer.\"\n\nAssess: How serious is it? (Data loss? Security? Minor display issue?) Does it affect other modules? When was it introduced?\n\nReview: Quality Management Plan — what is the defect severity classification? Was this defect covered by test cases?\n\nTake Action: Classify severity, raise a defect repair change request, communicate to customer based on severity, implement fix, regression test.\n\n→ WRONG first action: \"Immediately tell the customer.\" (Before you understand the severity and have a fix ready, this creates panic)\n\nScenario 3: \"The sponsor calls and says 'I heard the project is behind. Is it true?'\"\n\nAssess: Is it true? By how much? Do you have current data?\n\nReview: The latest Work Performance Report and EVM data. What does the data actually say?\n\nTake Action: Be honest. If behind: \"Yes, we are 2 weeks behind due to X. Here is our recovery plan: [specific actions]. We will be back on track by [date].\" If NOT behind: \"Our current data shows SPI=1.02 — we are slightly ahead. Here is the source of that information.\"\n\n→ WRONG action: \"Tell the sponsor everything is fine\" (dishonest if it is not, or confusing if you do not know yet)\n\nScenario 4: \"A vendor says they cannot deliver by the contracted date.\"\n\nAssess: How late? Why? Is this force majeure or vendor failure? What is the contract term? How does this affect the critical path?\n\nReview: Procurement Management Plan and the contract itself. What are the remedies, cure periods, and escalation paths?\n\nTake Action: Meet with the vendor face-to-face. Explore recovery options (expedite, partial delivery, substitute). If contractual breach — invoke remedies. Update risk register with new situation.\n\nScenario 5: \"Two stakeholders are lobbying for contradictory features.\"\n\nAssess: What are the actual requirements behind each position? Are they truly contradictory or just described differently?\n\nReview: Requirements Traceability Matrix. Which requirements have higher priority? What does the scope baseline say?\n\nTake Action: Facilitate a requirements workshop. Use MoSCoW or WSJF to objectively prioritise. If truly contradictory, escalate to sponsor for decision with options and trade-offs clearly presented.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — PMI \"Always/Never\" Pattern Recognition — 20 Quick Rules", "ALWAYS (almost always the correct answer):\n\n1. Assess BEFORE acting\n\n2. Communicate PROACTIVELY — before problems escalate\n\n3. Follow the PROCESS — even under pressure\n\n4. COLLABORATE before escalating\n\n5. Update the RISK REGISTER for new potential future events\n\n6. Update the ISSUE LOG for problems that have already occurred\n\n7. Get FORMAL WRITTEN acceptance before closing or transitioning\n\n8. Submit a CHANGE REQUEST — even for small changes\n\n9. Meet INDIVIDUALLY with conflicting parties before bringing them together\n\n10. Let the TEAM ESTIMATE (SMEs have the expertise, not the PM)\n\nNEVER (almost always the wrong answer):\n\n1. Never implement a change without CCB approval\n\n2. Never report status dishonestly — even under sponsor pressure\n\n3. Never fire/remove a team member as a FIRST response\n\n4. Never bypass the team's authority over their own work\n\n5. Never accept a gift from a vendor during procurement\n\n6. Never ignore a safety issue — STOP all work first\n\n7. Never skip lessons learned — even on a failed project\n\n8. Never release the team before formal acceptance is obtained\n\n9. Never use management reserve without management approval\n\n10. Never present AI-generated outputs without human validation\n\nThe pattern to recognize:\n\nIf an answer contains \"immediately,\" \"right away,\" \"without,\" or \"bypass\" — it is almost certainly wrong.\n\nIf an answer contains \"assess,\" \"review,\" \"communicate,\" \"collaborate,\" or \"formal\" — it is likely correct.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("The Complete PM Mindset Map — Every Scenario Type", tableHeaders = listOf("Scenario Type", "Wrong Answer Pattern", "Right Answer Pattern", "Key Principle"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Team member underperforming", "Fire/replace/escalate immediately", "Private conversation → understand root cause → coach/train/support", "Servant leadership, empathy"), listOf("Stakeholder dissatisfied", "Defensive, dismiss concern", "Meet with them, listen, understand concern, address it", "Collaboration, respect"), listOf("Scope change requested", "Just implement it (small) OR refuse outright", "Assess impact → raise formal change request → CCB decides", "Integrated change control"), listOf("Risk identified", "Panic or ignore", "Add to risk register → analyze → plan response", "Risk management is continuous"), listOf("Issue occurs", "Blame someone → escalate", "Update issue log → assess impact → take corrective action", "Accountability without blame"), listOf("Project behind schedule", "Work team harder (overtime)", "Assess critical path → compression options → transparent communication", "Sustainable pace + honest reporting"), listOf("Sponsor bypasses PM", "Just comply", "Professional conversation → agree on communication protocol", "Governance + respect"), listOf("Budget overrun", "Hide it", "Report accurately → assess EAC → present options to sponsor", "Honesty + proactive communication"), listOf("New technology requested mid-project", "Add it (exciting) OR refuse (not in scope)", "Formal change request → assess impact → CCB decides", "Change control + stakeholder management"), listOf("Team conflict", "Avoid OR force decision", "Meet individually → bring together → facilitate collaborative resolution", "Conflict resolution + servant leadership"), listOf("Safety issue identified", "Prioritise schedule over safety", "STOP all work → address safety → then assess project impact", "Safety ALWAYS first"), listOf("Regulatory change", "Ignore until forced", "Update risk register → assess compliance gap → change request", "Compliance is mandatory"), listOf("Vendor late", "Terminate immediately", "Assess contract → meet vendor → explore options → escalate if breach", "Contract management + collaboration"), listOf("Team member leaves mid-project", "Panic → hire externally immediately", "Assess impact → review resource plan → consider internal options first", "Resource management + contingency"), listOf("Quality defect post-acceptance", "Hide it", "Report to customer → raise defect repair change request → fix and re-test", "Honesty + quality management"))),
                s("Situational Leadership (Hersey & Blanchard) — Complete Reference", tableHeaders = listOf("Style", "Directive Behaviour", "Supportive Behaviour", "Team Member Profile", "Example"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("S1 — Directing/Telling", "High — specific instructions, close supervision", "Low — limited two-way communication", "Enthusiastic Beginner: HIGH commitment, LOW competence. New to role, wants to do well, does not know how.", "New graduate joining your agile team. Excited but has never used Jira, SCRUM, or your technology stack."), listOf("S2 — Coaching/Selling", "High — still structured and directive", "High — explains WHY, encourages, seeks input", "Disillusioned Learner: LOW commitment, some competence. Tried hard, hit setbacks, lost motivation.", "Developer who learned the tech but got burned on a failed sprint — now demotivated and questioning."), listOf("S3 — Supporting/Participating", "Low — hand over day-to-day decisions", "High — active listening, encouragement, facilitates problem-solving", "Capable but Cautious: HIGH competence, VARIABLE commitment. Knows the job but lacks confidence.", "Senior developer who can deliver but is new to the team and cautious about expressing opinions."), listOf("S4 — Delegating", "Low — minimal direction", "Low — low support; trusts the person completely", "Self-Reliant Achiever: HIGH competence, HIGH commitment. Expert who is motivated and confident.", "Your best developer who has delivered this type of module 10 times and owns the process completely."))),
                s("Situational Leadership (Hersey & Blanchard) — Complete Reference", "Common exam trap: A team in Tuckman \"Performing\" stage does NOT need S1 or S2. An experienced team with a new task needs S1/S2 for THAT TASK even if they are S4 overall. Match the style to BOTH the person AND the specific task.", SectionKind.WARN),
                s("Conflict Resolution — Deep Reference with Real Examples", tableHeaders = listOf("TKI Mode", "PMBOK Term", "When CORRECT", "When WRONG", "Real Example"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Collaborating", "Problem Solve", "Time available; both concerns important; relationship matters; novel problem needing best solution", "Emergency; other party acting in bad faith; trivial issue wasting time", "Two architects disagree on database design — schedule allows for a 2-hour workshop to define criteria and find the best approach"), listOf("Compromising", "Reconcile", "Equal power; time pressure moderate; temporary solution acceptable; both parties must win something", "One party is clearly right; a principle is at stake; the compromise itself creates a bad outcome", "Sprint scope dispute: team wants 5 features, stakeholder wants 8. Agree on 6 with 2 deferred to next sprint."), listOf("Accommodating", "Smooth", "Preserving harmony is more important than winning this point; you are wrong; building goodwill for bigger battle later", "You are right about something critical; giving in sets a dangerous precedent; issue is ethical", "Technical approach disagreement: you defer to the senior engineer's preference to preserve the relationship, even though you slightly prefer the alternative"), listOf("Competing", "Force", "Safety emergency; time is completely critical; unpopular but ethically necessary decision", "Ongoing working relationship needed; used routinely (creates resentment); other party has valid points", "At 5 PM the night before go-live, the team debates approach. PM makes the call: \"We go with Option A. Here's why. Let's move.\""), listOf("Avoiding", "Withdraw", "Trivial issue not worth the energy; both parties need time to cool down; you cannot win and costs are high", "Important issue; leaving it unresolved causes bigger problems later; avoidance is your habit (not a choice)", "Two team members snap at each other near deadline. PM says \"Let's finish the sprint and talk through this on Friday when we're less stressed.\""))),
                s("Communication — The PM Spends 90% of Time Here", tableHeaders = listOf("Communication Dimension", "Options", "Best For", "Exam Application"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Method", "Interactive (2-way real-time), Push (1-way sender), Pull (recipient accesses)", "Interactive = sensitive/complex. Push = routine updates. Pull = large audiences, reference material", "Conflict → Interactive. Status report → Push. Knowledge base → Pull."), listOf("Formality", "Formal (written, official) vs Informal (verbal, casual)", "Formal for baselines, contracts, official approvals. Informal for day-to-day coordination.", "Change approval → Formal. Daily standup → Informal."), listOf("Direction", "Upward (to sponsor/executives), Downward (to team), Lateral (to peers), External (to vendors/customers)", "All directions need different tone, format, and content", "Executive report ≠ team standup ≠ vendor email"), listOf("Tone", "Formal, semi-formal, conversational", "Match to relationship, culture, and sensitivity of topic", "Delivering bad news → formal, in-person. Celebrating a win → conversational."), listOf("Medium", "Face-to-face, video, phone, email, instant message, report, presentation", "Sensitive topics → highest bandwidth (face-to-face). Routine → lowest bandwidth (email).", "The MORE important and sensitive the message → the MORE bandwidth required"))),
                s("Stakeholder Communication — Tailoring by Type", tableHeaders = listOf("Stakeholder Type", "Information They Need", "Frequency", "Format", "Medium"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Executive Sponsor", "Status (RAG), risks, decisions needed, financial health, strategic alignment", "Monthly or at key milestones", "1-page executive summary with dashboard", "In-person or video"), listOf("Steering Committee", "Phase gate status, go/no-go decisions, portfolio impact, major risks", "At phase gates or quarterly", "Formal presentation with supporting data", "Meeting + slide deck"), listOf("Functional Managers", "Resource utilisation, upcoming demands, skills needed", "Monthly", "Resource loading report", "Email + calendar blocks"), listOf("End Users", "What is changing, how it affects them, training timeline, support resources", "Monthly through launch; then decreasing", "Change management newsletter, FAQs, demos", "Email + intranet + in-person sessions"), listOf("Team Members", "Sprint goals, impediments, decisions, retrospective actions, recognition", "Daily (standup) + weekly", "Kanban board, standup, team meeting", "In-person or video standup"), listOf("Vendors/Contractors", "Milestone status, payments, scope changes, issues, deliverable acceptance", "Weekly or at milestones", "Formal status report, meeting minutes", "Email + meetings"), listOf("Regulators", "Compliance status, audit readiness, regulatory deliverable schedule", "As required by regulation", "Formal regulatory report", "Written submission"))),
                s("Emotional Intelligence — Full Framework for the Exam", tableHeaders = listOf("EI Component", "Definition", "PM Situation", "High EI Response", "Low EI Response"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Self-Awareness", "Understanding your own emotions, triggers, biases, and impact on others", "You realise you are annoyed at a team member who keeps asking \"obvious\" questions", "Recognise the annoyance, reflect on why (impatience?), choose to respond with patience", "Snap at the team member: \"I already explained this!\""), listOf("Self-Regulation", "Managing your emotional reactions; thinking before acting", "The sponsor publicly criticises your project in front of the steering committee", "Breathe. Stay calm. Thank them for the feedback. Address specifics after the meeting.", "Defend yourself emotionally on the spot, damaging the relationship"), listOf("Motivation", "Internal drive to achieve; resilience; optimism", "The project hits a major setback — critical vendor backs out", "Reframe: \"This is a problem we will solve. Let us assess options.\"", "Give up or become paralysed by the setback"), listOf("Empathy", "Understanding and considering others' feelings and perspectives", "A team member delivers work late — you know they are going through a divorce", "Address the work issue, but do so with sensitivity. Ask how you can help.", "Issue a formal warning without acknowledging the personal context"), listOf("Social Skills", "Managing relationships, building networks, finding common ground", "Two department heads are feuding, creating project obstacles", "Facilitate a meeting that acknowledges both perspectives and finds shared interest", "Take a side or avoid the conflict entirely"))),
                s("The Golden Decision Rules — Memorise These", tableHeaders = listOf("Rule", "What It Means", "Example"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Assess before act", "Never take action (report, escalate, implement) before first understanding the full situation", "Vendor may be late → first assess impact on critical path, THEN decide what to do"), listOf("Process over authority", "The correct process takes precedence over who is requesting the change or shortcut", "Even the CEO cannot bypass integrated change control"), listOf("Communicate proactively", "Tell stakeholders about problems before they find out themselves", "If the project will miss a milestone, the PM tells the sponsor — the sponsor does not discover it"), listOf("Collaborate before escalate", "Try to resolve at the lowest level before involving higher authority", "Team conflict → resolve at team level → then PM level → then functional manager → then sponsor"), listOf("Plan for risks, not for issues", "Spend energy preventing problems, not recovering from them", "Identify vendor risk DURING planning → contingency plan → contingency reserve allocated"), listOf("Face-to-face for difficult conversations", "Sensitive, complex, conflict-related messages should be delivered in person (or video)", "Never fire someone via email. Never deliver major bad news via status report."), listOf("Safety stops everything", "A safety threat overrides all other priorities — schedule, budget, sponsor pressure", "A worker notices structural damage → stop work immediately, even if it delays go-live by 2 weeks"), listOf("Team estimates, PM integrates", "SMEs estimate; PM collects, integrates, and communicates", "PM does not estimate task durations — the person doing the task does"), listOf("Formal acceptance before closure", "You cannot close a project, phase, or deliverable without written acceptance from the authorised stakeholder", "Customer verbally says \"looks good\" → still need formal sign-off before closing"), listOf("Benefits owner, not PM, tracks post-closure", "After the project closes, the business-side benefits owner tracks realisation", "PM ensures the tracking mechanism is in place and transitions it — does not own it post-closure"))),
            )
        ),
        Chapter(
            "integration",
            "🔗 Integration Management (Governance Domain)",
            "Change Control · Project Documents · WPD→WPI→WPR · Project Closure",
            "Knowledge domains",
            listOf(
                s("Integration in PMBOK 8", "Integration Management — now part of the PMBOK 8 Governance performance domain — is the glue that holds all other domains together. The PM's primary role IS integration: ensuring all components work coherently toward project objectives."),
                s("Project Charter — Everything to Know", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "Detail"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Purpose", "Formally authorises the project and grants the PM authority to apply organisational resources"), listOf("Created by", "The Sponsor — not the PM (PM may assist in drafting it)"), listOf("Created when", "During the Initiating Focus Area — before planning begins"), listOf("Key contents", "Project purpose and justification, measurable objectives, high-level requirements, high-level milestones, budget summary, key stakeholders, PM authority level, assumptions and constraints"), listOf("Guidance level", "High-level — refer to it when \"high-level guidance\" is needed in exam questions"))),
                s("Project Charter — Everything to Know", "Project Charter = PM's authority document. Without it, the PM has no authority. The Project Management Plan = detailed guidance. Know when to reference which.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("Project Management Plan", "• The master integrating document — consolidates all subsidiary management plans and baselines\n• Baselines: Scope Baseline (scope statement + WBS + WBS dictionary), Schedule Baseline, Cost Baseline\n• Once formally approved, ALL changes require Integrated Change Control — even \"minor\" ones\n• It is PROGRESSIVE — updated throughout the project, not just written once and forgotten\n• Subsidiary plans: scope, schedule, cost, quality, resource, communications, risk, procurement, stakeholder management plans"),
                s("Project Management Plan", "When \"overall guidance\" or \"detailed guidance\" is needed — refer to the Project Management Plan. When only \"high-level guidance\" is needed — refer to the Project Charter.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Integrated Change Control — The Most Tested Process", "Change Identified→Raise Change Request→Assess Impact (scope/schedule/cost/quality)→CCB Reviews→Approve or Reject→Update Plans & Baselines→Communicate Outcome"),
                s("Integrated Change Control — The Most Tested Process", "ALL changes go through ICC — no exceptions. Sponsor request, executive directive, \"minor\" addition — all require a formal change request and CCB approval. This is non-negotiable in PMI's world.", SectionKind.DANGER),
                s("Key Project Documents — Know the Differences", tableHeaders = listOf("Document", "Purpose", "When Updated"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Issue Log", "Tracks problems that have ALREADY occurred or are occurring", "First thing when any issue arises — before taking action"), listOf("Risk Register", "Documents identified risks, analysis, and planned responses", "New risk identified; risk status changes; during monitoring"), listOf("Assumption Log", "Documents project assumptions and constraints throughout", "Throughout planning and execution as assumptions are identified"), listOf("Lessons Learned Register", "Captures knowledge continuously — NOT just at project close", "When issues resolved; at phase gates; and at project closure"), listOf("Change Log", "Tracks all change requests and their decisions/status", "Each time a change request is submitted or decided"), listOf("Work Performance Data (WPD)", "Raw observations from executing: % complete, costs incurred, start/finish dates", "Continuously during executing — raw input"), listOf("Work Performance Information (WPI)", "Analyzed WPD — EVM metrics, SV, CV, SPI, CPI, variances", "During M&C after analyzing WPD"), listOf("Work Performance Reports (WPR)", "Formatted communication of WPI to stakeholders", "As per communications management plan frequency"))),
                s("Key Project Documents — Know the Differences", "Data → Information → Reports: WPD (raw) → analyzed into WPI → formatted into WPR for stakeholders. This sequence is tested.", SectionKind.NOTE),
                s("Project Closure — Sequence Matters", "Complete all work→Verify/validate deliverables→Get FORMAL stakeholder acceptance→Document lessons learned→Transition to operations→Release resources→Close contracts→Archive documents"),
                s("Project Closure — Sequence Matters", "Formal acceptance of deliverables MUST come BEFORE lessons learned, BEFORE transition to operations, and BEFORE disbanding the team. This sequence is frequently tested.", SectionKind.DANGER),
                s("Change Request Types — Know All Four", tableHeaders = listOf("Type", "Definition", "Example", "Requires CCB?"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Corrective Action", "Realign project performance with the plan (fixing a problem that exists NOW)", "Adding resources to catch up on schedule", "Yes"), listOf("Preventive Action", "Reduce probability of future negative consequences (preventing a problem)", "Adding a quality checkpoint before a known risky phase", "Yes"), listOf("Defect Repair", "Fix a nonconforming product component", "Rewriting a module that failed testing", "Yes"), listOf("Update", "Modify formally controlled documents or plans", "Updating the schedule baseline after approved scope change", "Yes"))),
                s("Change Request Types — Know All Four", "ALL four types go through Integrated Change Control. Even \"preventive action\" — which sounds harmless — requires formal approval because it changes the plan.", SectionKind.WARN),
                s("Change Control Board (CCB) — Who and What", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "Detail"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Who is on the CCB?", "Defined in the change management plan — typically sponsor, PM, key stakeholders, technical leads. The PM may or may not have voting authority."), listOf("What does CCB do?", "Reviews change requests; evaluates impact analysis; approves, rejects, or defers; records decisions"), listOf("Can PM approve changes alone?", "Only if explicitly authorised in the change management plan for specific types/thresholds. Otherwise — NO."), listOf("Emergency changes?", "Change management plan should define an expedited process. Even emergencies follow a (faster) process — they do not bypass it."), listOf("Change vs. Baseline", "Approved changes update the baseline. Rejected changes are logged with rationale. All are tracked in the Change Log."))),
                s("Configuration Management vs. Change Management", "Configuration Management\n • Manages the specifications of deliverables and processes\n• Identifies which items are under configuration control\n• Tracks versions, status, and audit trail of configuration items\n• Example: Version 2.3 of the design document is the approved baseline\nChange Management\n • Manages changes to baselines and project documents\n• Governs the process of raising, evaluating, approving changes\n• Change Control Board authority defined here\n• Example: A request to move from Version 2.3 to 2.4 must go through CCB"),
                s("Project Management Information System (PMIS)", "• Automated system used to collect, store, distribute project information\n• Includes: scheduling software, work authorisation systems, configuration management systems, information collection/distribution systems\n• PMBOK 8: PMIS increasingly leverages AI for real-time tracking, analysis, and reporting\n• Common exam point: PMIS is a TOOL/TECHNIQUE, not an output. It is an EEF (part of the organisational infrastructure)."),
                s("Phase Gates / Stage Gates", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "Detail"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Definition", "Review at end of a phase where a decision is made to continue, modify, or terminate the project"), listOf("Also called", "Kill points, milestones, phase reviews, decision gates, toll gates"), listOf("Who decides?", "Typically the sponsor or steering committee — not the PM alone"), listOf("What is reviewed?", "Deliverables, performance vs plan, benefits realisation progress, business case validity, risks"), listOf("PMBOK 8 view", "Phase gates are tailoring decisions — used more in predictive; adaptive uses sprint reviews as lightweight gates"))),
                s("Worked example — Real-World Example: Integrated Change Control in Practice", "Scenario: You are PM for a ₹4 crore core banking system upgrade at a regional bank. Mid-execution, the CTO walks into your war room and says: \"Our auditors need a new transaction audit trail module — add it to the project. We need it in 3 months.\"\n\nWhat the PM does (step by step):\n\n1. Listen and acknowledge — \"Understood. Let me assess the impact and come back to you by end of week.\"\n\n2. Raise a formal Change Request — document: description, justification, requestor\n\n3. Impact Analysis:\n\n   • Scope: ~800 hours of new development + testing\n\n   • Schedule: +6 weeks to the critical path (not 3 months as requested)\n\n   • Cost: +₹38 lakh (dev, testing, infra)\n\n   • Quality risk: Rushing audit trail = regulatory liability\n\n   • Risk: External audit dependency; data privacy compliance needed\n\n4. CCB Review: Present options — (A) Full audit trail in 10 weeks, +₹38L; (B) Basic audit trail in 6 weeks, +₹18L, deferred enhancements later\n\n5. CCB Decision: Option B approved\n\n6. Update baselines: Scope baseline (new WBS element), Schedule baseline (+6 weeks), Cost baseline (+₹18L)\n\n7. Communicate: Inform all stakeholders of the approved change and new dates\n\nWhat the PM does NOT do: Tell the team \"CTO said add it\" and start work without formal approval.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Project Documents — Decision Guide", "Question: \"A team member finds a new risk. What document do you update first?\"\n\nAnswer: Risk Register → then analyze → then plan response → then update the Risk Management Plan if the approach changes.\n\nQuestion: \"A vendor delivers a component late. What document do you update?\"\n\nAnswer: Issue Log (it has ALREADY happened) → then assess schedule impact → then corrective action/change request.\n\nQuestion: \"A sponsor's assumption about market conditions proves false. What do you update?\"\n\nAnswer: Assumption Log (assumption proved incorrect) → then Risk Register (if this creates new risks) → then review Business Case validity.\n\nMental model: Future uncertainty → Risk Register. Past/present problem → Issue Log. Assumption invalidated → Assumption Log. Process knowledge gained → Lessons Learned Register.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — WPD → WPI → WPR Flow — Fintech Example", "Example project: Mobile banking app, Sprint 14\n\nWork Performance DATA (WPD) — Raw facts collected during executing:\n\n• 8 of 12 planned stories completed\n\n• 142 hours logged, planned was 120 hours\n\n• 3 defects found in testing, 2 fixed, 1 open\n\n• Payment gateway integration still in progress\n\nWork Performance INFORMATION (WPI) — Analyzed during M&C:\n\n• SPI = EV/PV = (8/12) = 0.67 → 33% behind schedule\n\n• CPI = EV/AC = (8×10 points)/(142h) = needs baseline to compute in \$\n\n• Sprint Goal at risk: payment gateway at 0% with 3 days left\n\n• Root cause: dependency on vendor API documentation (delayed 5 days)\n\nWork Performance REPORT (WPR) — Formatted for stakeholders:\n\n• Executive Dashboard: \"Sprint 14 — Amber. Payment gateway delayed due to vendor dependency. Recovery plan: expedited API docs by Thursday, Saturday catch-up session. Expected completion: Monday.\"\n\n• Technical Report: Full defect log, story status, SPI/CPI, risk update\n\nKey point: The SAME raw data becomes different WPR formats for different audiences.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Project Charter — Every Field Explained", tableHeaders = listOf("Charter Element", "What It Contains", "Why It Matters on the Exam"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Project Purpose", "WHY this project exists — the business problem or opportunity it addresses", "The sponsor cannot authorise a project without a clear purpose — no purpose = no charter"), listOf("Measurable Project Objectives", "SMART objectives: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound", "Objectives are NOT deliverables. \"Reduce processing time by 40% by Q3\" is an objective."), listOf("High-Level Requirements", "Broad-level description of what is needed — NOT detailed requirements", "Detailed requirements come later in Scope. Charter = boundaries, not specifications."), listOf("High-Level Project Description", "Brief description of the project, approach, and deliverables at a summary level", "Enough to authorise without premature detail"), listOf("Overall Project Risk", "Initial assessment of uncertainty and risk at the programme level", "Acknowledges that all projects carry uncertainty — not a full risk register"), listOf("Summary Milestone Schedule", "Key dates and major milestones — not a full schedule", "Start date, key phase completions, go-live date, close date"), listOf("Pre-approved Financial Resources", "Initial budget authorisation — the amount the sponsor is committing", "The PM spends within this limit unless a change request is approved"), listOf("Project Approval Requirements", "What constitutes project success and who approves it", "Critical for Validate Scope and project closure"), listOf("Project Exit Criteria", "Conditions under which the project may be cancelled or closed early", "Rarely tested but important for governance questions"), listOf("Assigned PM and Authority Level", "Who is the PM and what decisions can they make independently", "This is the PM's authorisation document — without it, PM has no formal authority"), listOf("Sponsor Name and Authority", "Who authorised the project and can make strategic decisions", "Sponsor is the escalation path for issues beyond PM authority"))),
                s("Project Charter — Every Field Explained", "The project charter is signed by the SPONSOR (or initiating entity), not the PM. The PM may WRITE the charter but does not AUTHORISE it. This is frequently tested.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("Project Management Plan — All Component Plans", tableHeaders = listOf("Component Plan", "What It Governs", "Key Contents"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Scope Management Plan", "How scope is defined, developed, monitored, and controlled", "WBS creation approach, Scope statement development process, Validate Scope process, Change control for scope"), listOf("Requirements Management Plan", "How requirements are collected, analysed, documented, and managed", "Elicitation approach, Prioritisation method, RTM structure, Requirements approval process"), listOf("Schedule Management Plan", "How the schedule is developed, monitored, and controlled", "Scheduling model, Estimation approach, Float thresholds, Schedule update process"), listOf("Cost Management Plan", "How costs are estimated, budgeted, and controlled", "Estimation techniques, Level of precision, Control thresholds (EVM triggers), Reporting format"), listOf("Quality Management Plan", "How quality is defined, assured, and controlled", "Quality standards, Metrics, Test approach, DoD in agile, COQ targets"), listOf("Resource Management Plan", "How physical and human resources are planned, acquired, managed, released", "Team structure, Roles and responsibilities (RACI), Resource calendars, Recognition plan"), listOf("Communications Management Plan", "Who receives what information, in what format, how often, through which channel", "Stakeholder communication matrix, Meeting schedule, Escalation path"), listOf("Risk Management Plan", "How risk management is conducted throughout the project", "Risk categories, Probability/Impact scales, Risk appetite, Reserve approach, Review frequency"), listOf("Procurement Management Plan", "How procurement is planned, executed, and controlled", "Contract types to be used, Vendor evaluation criteria, Contracting process, Relationship management"), listOf("Stakeholder Engagement Plan", "How stakeholders will be engaged to ensure their needs are met", "Engagement strategy per stakeholder group, Tactics for moving from current to desired engagement level"), listOf("Change Management Plan", "How changes are requested, evaluated, approved, and implemented", "Change request form, CCB composition, Authority levels, Turnaround times"), listOf("Configuration Management Plan", "How project artifacts are versioned, controlled, and maintained", "Version control approach, Baseline management, Audit procedures"))),
                s("Change Request Types — Complete Guide", tableHeaders = listOf("Type", "Definition", "Example", "Goes Through CCB?"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Corrective Action", "Realigns project performance with the plan — fixes a current deviation", "Project is 2 weeks behind schedule. Corrective action: add resources to critical path activities.", "Yes — if it affects baselines; No — if within PM authority"), listOf("Preventive Action", "Reduces probability of a future negative risk event before it occurs", "Risk analysis shows vendor delay likely. Preventive action: begin qualifying backup vendor now.", "Yes — if it affects baselines; No — if within PM authority"), listOf("Defect Repair", "Fixes a defect in a project component that does not meet requirements", "A module fails performance testing. Defect repair: recode the database query to meet <2 second SLA.", "Usually yes — affects work and possibly schedule/cost"), listOf("Updates", "Formal revision to project documents or baselines", "Scope statement updated to reflect customer-approved feature addition", "Yes — any baseline update requires CCB approval"))),
                s("Change Request Types — Complete Guide", "CORRECTIVE vs PREVENTIVE: Corrective addresses CURRENT deviation (something already went wrong). Preventive addresses FUTURE risk (prevent something from going wrong). Both are formally submitted as change requests if they affect baselines.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Integrated Change Control — The Complete Process", tableHeaders = listOf("Step", "Who", "Action", "Output"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("1. Identify Need", "Anyone on the project", "Recognise that a change may be needed (scope discovery, risk event, regulatory requirement)", "Change trigger"), listOf("2. Submit Change Request", "Requestor (any stakeholder, team member, PM, or sponsor)", "Complete and submit the Change Request Form with full description and reason", "Submitted Change Request"), listOf("3. Impact Assessment", "PM + relevant SMEs", "Analyse impact on: Scope, Schedule, Cost, Quality, Risk, Resources, and Stakeholder satisfaction", "Impact Analysis document"), listOf("4. CCB Review", "Change Control Board", "Review the request, impact analysis, and alternatives; apply governance authority levels", "CCB meeting minutes"), listOf("5. CCB Decision", "CCB", "Approve, Reject, Defer, or request more information", "Change Request decision (Approved / Rejected / Deferred)"), listOf("6. Update PM Plan", "PM", "Update ALL affected component plans and baselines to reflect the approved change", "Updated Project Management Plan and Baselines"), listOf("7. Communicate", "PM", "Notify all relevant stakeholders of the decision and what changes", "Communication to stakeholders"), listOf("8. Implement", "PM / Team", "Execute the approved change as described in the updated plan", "Updated Deliverables, Work Performance Data"), listOf("9. Monitor & Control", "PM", "Track that the change has been implemented correctly and verify the expected outcome", "Change Log entry closed, Work Performance Information"))),
                s("Project Documents — Master Reference Table", tableHeaders = listOf("Document", "What It Contains", "Updated During", "Used In"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Activity List", "All schedule activities with descriptions", "Planning, when scope changes", "Schedule Development"), listOf("Activity Attributes", "Constraints, leads, lags, resources per activity", "Planning", "Schedule Development, Resource Planning"), listOf("Assumption Log", "All assumptions and constraints; updated when assumptions prove true/false", "Throughout project", "Risk identification, planning decisions"), listOf("Basis of Estimates", "How estimates were derived; confidence level; range", "Planning", "Change request evaluation, audit"), listOf("Change Log", "All change requests and their status (submitted/approved/rejected/implemented)", "Whenever a CR is raised or decided", "Status reporting, audit, lessons learned"), listOf("Cost Forecasts", "EAC, ETC, VAC calculations", "Monitoring & Controlling", "Status reporting, sponsor decision-making"), listOf("Duration Estimates", "O/M/P or single-point estimates for activities", "Planning", "Schedule development"), listOf("Issue Log", "Current problems (already occurred), ownership, resolution status", "Executing and M&C", "Status meetings, escalation"), listOf("Lessons Learned Register", "Knowledge from current project for future use", "Throughout — not just at close", "Future project planning, OPA updates"), listOf("Milestone List", "Key dates and milestones with completion criteria", "Planning, when schedule changes", "Schedule, stakeholder communication"), listOf("Physical Resource Assignments", "Equipment, materials, facilities allocated to activities", "Planning and Executing", "Resource tracking"), listOf("Project Calendar", "Working days, holidays, resource availability patterns", "Planning", "Schedule development"), listOf("Project Communications", "All formal communications produced", "Executing", "Audit, stakeholder management"), listOf("Project Schedule", "Gantt or other schedule format with dates and assignments", "Planning and updates", "Status reporting, team coordination"), listOf("Project Team Assignments", "People assigned to activities with roles", "Planning and Executing", "Schedule development, responsibility"), listOf("Quality Control Measurements", "Results of quality inspections and tests", "Control Quality process", "Manage Quality, defect analysis"), listOf("Quality Metrics", "Specific parameters to measure (e.g., defect density, test coverage)", "Planning — Quality Management Plan", "Control Quality process"), listOf("Quality Reports", "Summary of quality activities, issues, and improvements", "Manage Quality process", "Sponsor communication, audits"), listOf("Requirements Documentation", "All requirements with attributes and approval status", "Planning (Elicit Requirements)", "Scope definition, RTM"), listOf("Requirements Traceability Matrix", "Links requirements to WBS elements, test cases, deliverables", "Planning and when requirements change", "Validate Scope, change impact"), listOf("Resource Breakdown Structure", "Hierarchical chart of resources by category and type", "Planning", "Resource cost estimation"), listOf("Resource Calendars", "Availability of specific resources over the project timeline", "Planning and Executing", "Schedule development, resource levelling"), listOf("Resource Requirements", "Type and quantity of resources needed per activity", "Planning", "Resource acquisition, cost estimation"), listOf("Risk Register", "All identified risks with analysis, response plans, and owners", "Throughout project", "Risk monitoring, contingency planning"), listOf("Risk Report", "Summary of overall risk exposure and key individual risks", "Risk analysis processes", "Steering committee, executive communication"), listOf("Schedule Forecasts", "SPI, projected finish date, schedule variance", "M&C", "Sponsor reports, decision-making"), listOf("Stakeholder Register", "All stakeholders with their role, interest, influence, and engagement level", "Initiating, then updated continuously", "Stakeholder management, communication planning"), listOf("Team Charter", "Team norms, values, working agreements, communication preferences", "Forming stage (early executing)", "Conflict resolution reference, new member onboarding"))),
